Five hundred years is both an unimaginable distance and a surprisingly short moment in the story of humanity.

If someone from the year 1526 could visit our world today, they would encounter a civilization that would appear almost magical. They would see machines that fly, voices traveling instantly across continents, tiny devices that contain the knowledge of thousands of generations, and humans extending their reach beyond the boundaries of Earth itself. Our world would seem less like an evolution of their civilization and more like a different species had inherited the planet.

So the question becomes: what would someone from the year 2526 think of us?

Would they see us as pioneers who laid the foundation for a remarkable future? Or would they look back at our era as a civilization trapped in its infancy—one that possessed incredible knowledge but lacked the wisdom to use it?
